// T4, the switch precision cliff: a function containing a `switch` releases its
// function-scope locals.
//
// BEFORE THE FOUR ARMS THIS RECLAIMED NOTHING. release_decision's walk met
// `switch` at its `default:` arm, marked the WHOLE function unreadable, and every
// local in it refused with RELEASE_NO_UNKNOWN -- including `p`, which is an
// obvious candidate at function scope with a clean allocation. Ordinary Goo code
// contains a switch frequently, so that was the widest precision limit T4 had
// left. release_decision_test row 17 records the flip.
//
// `p` IS THE SUBJECT, not the switch. Condition 5 refuses anything declared
// INSIDE a case body, because the slot is hoisted to the entry block while the
// initialising store stays at the declaration site (see
// examples/arc_release_cond_probe.goo). What this probe proves is narrower and
// is the actual feature: the mere PRESENCE of a switch no longer poisons the
// function.
//
// The switch has a `default:` clause on purpose. The parser builds that as an
// AST_CASE_CLAUSE with NULL exprs rather than an AST_DEFAULT_CLAUSE, and the arm
// refuses any case node of an unexpected kind, so a default clause exercises that
// path rather than tripping it.
//
// NOTHING IS READ THROUGH `p` INTO A GLOBAL. escape_expr_taint's AST_UNARY_EXPR
// arm returns the operand's taint for every unary operator, so `sink = sink + *p`
// would mark `p` escaping and this probe would reclaim nothing while still
// passing. examples/arc_release_probe.goo records the same arm as the reason it
// increments a counter.
//
// IMPORT-FREE, for the reason the other arc probes record.
package main

var sink int

func classify(n int) {
	p := new(int)
	*p = n
	switch n % 3 {
	case 0:
		sink = sink + 1
	case 1:
		sink = sink + 2
	default:
		sink = sink + 3
	}
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		classify(i)
	}
}
